All 6 tests pass.

`src/str.js:7`:

```js
function truncate(s, n) {
  if (s.length <= n) return s;
  return s.slice(0, n) + '…';
}
```

Two judgment calls worth flagging, since you may want them the other way:

- **Result length is `n + 1`.** I read "cuts to n characters and appends an ellipsis" literally, so `truncate('abcdef', 3)` is `'abc…'`. The common alternative is budgeting the ellipsis inside `n` (`'ab…'`), which matters if `n` is a hard display width. Say the word and I'll flip it.
- **Counting is UTF-16 code units,** inherited from `.length`/`.slice` — same as the existing `trim`. Emoji and other astral characters can be cut mid-pair, producing a lone surrogate. Fixing that means `[...s]` and a cost change from O(1) to O(length). Not worth it for ASCII-ish input; worth it if this touches user-generated text.

No input validation on `n` — a non-number makes the comparison false and `slice` coerce, so `truncate('abc', undefined)` returns `'…'` silently. Nothing else in this file validates either, so I matched the surrounding style rather than introducing a lone guard.

